          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "തൊപ്പി — toppi — a cap."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ഇത് പുതിയ തൊപ്പിയാണ്. — itŭ putiya toppiyāṇŭ — \"This is a new cap.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four things worn, and only two endings between them. തൊപ്പി (toppi) ends in ഇ, so it joins the way സാരി (sāri) does:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "ends in",
                "word",
                "joined to ആണ്"
              ],
              "columns": 3,
              "rowCount": 4,
              "utterances": [
                "ends in: half-u. word: മുണ്ട് (muṇṭŭ). joined to ആണ്: മുണ്ടാണ് (muṇṭāṇŭ).",
                "ends in: half-u. word: ചെരുപ്പ് (ceruppŭ). joined to ആണ്: ചെരുപ്പാണ് (ceruppāṇŭ).",
                "ends in: ഇ. word: സാരി. joined to ആണ്: സാരിയാണ് (sāriyāṇŭ).",
                "ends in: ഇ. word: തൊപ്പി. joined to ആണ്: തൊപ്പിയാണ് (toppiyāṇŭ)."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nothing in this chapter asked you to learn a join. Both are long-standing: എന്താണ് has taken the half-u since your very first question, and കുട്ടി has taken the യ since ഇത് എന്റെ കുട്ടിയാണ്. പാട്ട്, കളി and ഭംഗി are the most recent words to use them, not the words they began with. What the chapter gave you is four things to wear."
            }
          ]
        },
